<div class="header"><h2>history</h2></div>
<div class="body">
<h3>Discovery in Japan (1908)</h3>
<p>Japanese chemist <strong>Dr. Kikunae Ikeda</strong> first identified the compound while analyzing kombu (kelp) dashi broth. He recognized a distinct savory taste different from sweet, salty, sour, and bitter, and isolated glutamate crystals as its source. Ikeda named this taste "umami" (Japanese for "delicious savory"). By 1909, he and business partner Saburosuke Suzuki developed mass production methods and launched MSG under the brand name "Ajinomoto" <sup><a href="#src-history-1">history-1</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>Spread to China (1918)</h3>
<p>Ajinomoto began selling MSG in China in 1918, marketing it as part of a modern lifestyle. Some Chinese consumers viewed it suspiciously as a symbol of Japanese imperialism. A Chinese competitor, <strong>Tian Chu (Heavenly Kitchen)</strong>, entered the market promoting a domestic alternative, along with a "Buddha's Hand" brand targeting Buddhist vegetarians. MSG became popular as an affordable instant stock and flavor enhancer for vegetarian meals by the late 1930s <sup><a href="#src-history-2">history-2</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>Introduction to the United States</h3>
<p>MSG arrived in the U.S. through <strong>Hawaii in the 1920s</strong>, likely via Japanese immigrants working on sugar plantations. Early marketing attempts to American housewives in the 1920s failed. Americans were introduced to MSG in the <strong>1930s-1940s</strong> through Chinese restaurants in emerging Chinatowns. After World War II, the U.S. military adopted MSG to improve the flavor of combat rations. By the <strong>1950s</strong>, MSG was already widely used in packaged foods across the U.S., including canned soups, frozen dinners, snacks, and baby food <sup><a href="#src-history-2">history-2</a>, <a href="#src-history-3">history-3</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>Europe: From Taboo to Table</h3>
<p>MSG reached Europe shortly after its introduction to China, though it remained controversial for decades. In the <strong>1970s-1980s</strong>, European fine dining largely avoided MSG, viewing it as a "cheap" additive. When Michelin-starred chef <strong>Jean-Claude Bourgueil</strong> admitted to using MSG in his three-star Dusseldorf restaurant "Im Schiffchen," the <em>Guide Gault-Millau</em> condemned him and temporarily removed the restaurant from its ratings. The restaurant subsequently lost its third star <sup><a href="#src-history-7">history-7</a></sup>.</p>
<p>European restaurants traditionally did <strong>not</strong> keep MSG shakers on tables like salt and pepper. Unlike Asian restaurants where MSG is commonly offered, European dining has long relied on natural umami sources (aged cheeses, mushrooms, stocks). However, the stigma is fading. Following endorsements from chefs like <strong>David Chang</strong> (Momofuku) and growing scientific evidence of MSG safety, some European restaurants now openly use MSG <sup><a href="#src-history-7">history-7</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Today, MSG is available in European supermarkets (e.g., Ajinomoto brand) and specialty food shops, though it remains less common in home kitchens than in the U.S. or Asia. Some European restaurants still advertise "no additives" or "no MSG" as a selling point <sup><a href="#src-history-7">history-7</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>Lawsuits and Controversy</h3>
<p><strong>1968</strong> saw the birth of "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" when Dr. Robert Ho Man Kwok wrote a letter to the <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> describing symptoms including radiating pain in his arms, weakness, and heart palpitations after eating at Chinese restaurants. This spawned widespread fear and media attention <sup><a href="#src-history-3">history-3</a></sup>.</p>
<p>In <strong>1969</strong>, Dr. John W. Olney, a neuroscientist at Washington University, published research showing MSG caused brain lesions in infant mice, particularly in the hypothalamus. He testified before the Senate Select Committee on Nutrition and Human Needs chaired by Senator George McGovern. Under public pressure, major baby food manufacturers (Gerber, Beech-Nut, Heinz) voluntarily removed MSG from baby food by October 1969 <sup><a href="#src-history-4">history-4</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The FDA conducted a formal analysis of adverse reactions to MSG in <strong>1995</strong> <sup><a href="#src-history-5">history-5</a></sup>. In <strong>2015</strong>, a $1.5 million settlement was reached in an MSG labeling dispute <sup><a href="#src-history-6">history-6</a></sup>.</p>
